  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Bongo\"\nsubtitle: \"South Sudan\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Bongo people |\n| Where | Bahr al Ghazal region, South Sudan |\n| How many | Not specified |\n| Language | Bongo (also Bungu or Dor), a Central Sudanic tonal language |\n| Religion | Not well documented; some Christian influence |\n| Known for | Carved wooden stools and grave figures · Ivory and iron work · Hunting horns and musical instruments · Wooden lip ornaments and beaded necklaces · Iron currency blades |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Bongo are a sparse population living in South Sudan's Bahr al Ghazal region who speak a tonal Central Sudanic language with a quinary-vigesimal counting system. Most knowledge of Bongo culture comes from nineteenth-century travelers' word lists and museum collections of objects rather than from recent ethnographic study. The surviving record focuses on material goods—carved wooden items, weapons, instruments, and ornaments—that reveal skilled work in wood, bone, and metal.\n\n## Material culture\n\nBongo material culture centers on carved wood, worked ivory, and iron, particularly in stools, hunting gear, musical instruments, and personal ornament.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nNo textile traditions are documented in available sources.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nGarments are not described, but women wore distinctive wooden lip ornaments along with ivory armlets, necklaces, and a skin pad worn on the head for carrying loads.\n\n- **Wooden lip ornaments** — Carved in wood and painted black, worn by women.\n- **Ivory armlets** — Bracelets of worked ivory worn as personal adornment.\n- **Head pad** — A circle of skin with sandy-colored hair around the edge, worn on the head to carry loads.\n\n### Architecture\n\nHouse structure is not described, but carved wooden ornaments decorated huts.\n\n- **Bird hut ornament** — Solid wood carving of a bird with a collar around its neck, originally painted brown and yellow, with a red eye bead inlaid.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nIron blades and carved wooden pieces—especially stools—dominate household and working equipment.\n\n- **Hoe currency** — An iron blade shaped like an ace of spades with a long handle, used as a form of money.\n- **Decorated iron knife** — An oval blade with punched decoration on one side.\n- **Smoking pipe** — Made from a clay bowl, bamboo stem, and gourd mouthpiece partly covered with skin.\n- **Tusk seal-stamp** — Carved from an elephant tusk end with its face engraved in intersecting lines.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nWomen wore carved wooden lip ornaments and necklaces strung with ivory pieces and berries, with no records of tattooing or scarification.\n\n- **Cowrie-shell ivory necklace** — Ten flat oval ivory pieces carved to resemble cowrie shells strung on fiber with a flat ivory disc.\n- **Necklace of conjuring beads** — Five strings of twisted fiber strung with round brown berries, with two transverse pieces of hide, used for ritual purposes.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nBongo musicians played drums, a harp-like lyre, and multiple kinds of horns carved from wood, bone, or ivory.\n\n- **Tom-tom drum** — A hollowed wooden log open at both ends with skin heads held by skin thongs.\n- **Harp** — A semi-circular sounding chamber with wood back covered in skin parchment, a neck of two sticks joined by a bar, and gut strings.\n- **Ivory hunting horn** — Made from an elephant tusk end, carved with incised lines and pricked dots.\n- **Wooden hunting horn** — A carved wood horn used for hunting.\n- **Ivory trumpet** — An ivory instrument for performance or signaling.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nNo information is available about Bongo dance or dramatic performance.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nFunerary commemoration is the best-documented ritual, involving carved wooden grave figures and commemorative posts.\n\n- **Ngya** — A commemorative post carved in mahogany and erected for the dead.\n- **Grave figure** — Life-size standing wooden figures of men and women, carved in wood or red wood and placed at graves.\n\n## Foodways\n\nNo Bongo staples, dishes, or cooking methods are documented.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nNo folktales, epics, or proverbs are recorded, but the Bongo language itself has been documented through vocabularies and grammars since the nineteenth century.\n\n- **Bongo Grammar Book** — Produced in 2018 by Bongo authors with SIL-South Sudan, including a companion vowel and consonant book.\n- **Bongo language documentation** — Vocabularies gathered by Petherick (1861), von Heuglin (1869), Schweinfurth (1873), Evans-Pritchard (1937), and Kilpatrick (1985).\n\n## Language & religion\n\nBongo is a tonal Central Sudanic language with ten vowel qualities and a quinary-vigesimal counting system; religious practice is largely undocumented except for Christian influence.\n\n- **Tonal system** — The language uses high, mid, low, and falling tones to distinguish meaning.\n- **Vowel harmony** — Five heavy vowels and five light vowels interact in words according to harmonic rules.\n- **Jesus Film translation** — The Bible film has been translated into Bongo, indicating Christian missionary activity.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Bongo* — The language and people of Bahr al Ghazal, South Sudan\n- *Bungu* — Alternative name for the Bongo language\n- *Dor* — Another name for the Bongo language\n- *Central Sudanic* — Language family to which Bongo belongs\n- *Tonal language* — A language where pitch distinguishes meaning\n- *Quinary-vigesimal* — Counting system based on five and twenty\n- *Vowel harmony* — Vowel types in a word must match certain patterns\n- *Ngya* — Commemorative post carved for the dead\n- *Hoe currency* — Iron blade shaped like an ace of spades used as money\n- *Lip ornament* — Wooden carved piece worn in the lip by women\n- *Tom-tom* — Hollowed log drum with skin heads\n- *Cowrie shell* — Small sea shell shape imitated in ivory carvings\n- *Conjuring beads* — Ritual necklace of berries and hide with spiritual purpose\n- *Bahr al Ghazal* — Region in South Sudan where the Bongo live\n- *Mahogany* — Type of wood used for carving commemorative posts\n- *Ivory trumpet* — Wind instrument carved from elephant tusk\n- *Hide* — Animal skin used for shields, quivers, and other objects\n- *Thongs* — Strips of leather or skin used to hold drum heads\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Crystal, Kathryn; Armand, Matthew; Armand, Breanna. *Sociolinguistic Survey of the Bongo of South Sudan.* SIL Electronic Survey Reports 2020–010. Dallas: SIL International, 2020.\n- Moi, Daniel Rabbi; Kuduku, Mario Lau Babur; Michael, Sister Mary Mangira; John, Simon Hagimir; Mafoi, Rapheal Zakenia Paul; Kuduku, Nyoul Gulluma. *Bongo Grammar Book*, 3rd trial ed. Juba: SIL-South Sudan, 2018. (With the companion *Bongo Consonant and Vowel Book*, 2018.)\n- Santandrea, Stefano. *A Small Comparative Vocabulary of Bongo Baka Yulu Kara.* Rome: Sodality of St Peter Claver, 1963.\n- Thayer, Linda Jean. *A Reconstructed History of the Chari Languages: Comparative Bongo-Bagirmi-Sara Segmental Phonology With Evidence From Arabic Loanwords.* PhD thesis, University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign, 1974.\n- Schweinfurth, Georg August. *Linguistische Ergebnisse, Einer Reise Nach Centralafrika*, 1873; Petherick, John. *Egypt, the Soudan, and Central Africa*, 1861; von Heuglin, Theodor. *Reise in das Gebiet des Weissen Nil, &c. 1862-1864*, 1869 — the three earliest Bongo vocabularies. Later documentation by E. E. Evans-Pritchard (word lists, 1937) and Eileen Kilpatrick (phonology, 1985).\n- Wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bongo_language\n- Bongo at Gurtong, and Bongo at WALS Online (typological database entry).\n- Metropolitan Museum of Art online collection — *Ngya* (commemorative post), mahogany, late 19th century: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection\n- British Museum online collection (the weapons, stools, horns, drum, harp, ornaments, currency blades and grave figures described above): https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection\n\n",
